ask(1456,'tfidf-math','Under the unsmoothed printed IDF with natural logs, how should ubiquitous and unseen terms be handled?', ['A term in every document has IDF 0; a term in no document makes ln(N/df) undefined','A term in every document has IDF 1; an unseen term has IDF 0','A term in every document has IDF ln N; an unseen term has IDF 1','Both cases have IDF 0 because neither distinguishes documents'],['df=N gives ln 1=0; df=0 gives division by zero and is outside this formula’s domain.','The ubiquitous-term value 1 belongs to an additive-one convention, not this equation.','Substituting df=N does not give ln N, and df=0 is not defined.','A useful interpretation does not repair division by zero without an explicit convention.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Substitute df=N: ln(N/N)=0.','Substitute df=0: N/0 is undefined.','Smoothing or vocabulary restrictions must be separately specified.'],formulas=[TFF],cites=[(1456,'equation','IDF ratio and product formula')],ass=['N is positive; the unseen-term case intentionally tests the domain boundary.'])
ask(1456,'tfidf-math',CORP+' Using exactly the printed TF=count/document-length and IDF=ln(N/df), what are d1’s weights for ball and red?', ['ln(3)/5 ≈ 0.21972 and ln(3/2)/5 ≈ 0.08109','ln(3) ≈ 1.09861 and ln(3/2) ≈ 0.40547','ln(3)/8 ≈ 0.13733 and ln(3/2)/8 ≈ 0.05068','ln(3/2)/5 ≈ 0.08109 and ln(3)/5 ≈ 0.21972'],['Both counts are one in a five-token document, but ball appears in one corpus document and red in two.','These omit the required division by document length.','Eight is vocabulary size, not d1’s token count.','This exchanges the document frequencies of ball and red.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['d1 has five tokens, so both TF values are 1/5.','df(ball)=1 and df(red)=2 among N=3 documents.','Multiply each TF by its natural-log IDF to obtain approximately .21972 and .08109.'],formulas=[TFF],cites=[(1454,'code','Three documents'),(1455,'equation','Normalized TF'),(1456,'equation','Unsmoothed IDF and TF-IDF product')],ass=['No smoothing, additive constant or final vector normalization; natural logarithm explicitly supplied.'])
ask(1457,'tfidf-code','Why does the source’s default TfidfVectorizer table differ from directly applying the two preceding equations?', ['The default code smooths IDF, adds one and L2-normalizes weighted count vectors','The code uses the same equations but changes the logarithm from natural to base ten only','The code replaces all counts with POS-tag frequencies','The code counts every repeated term as a separate document'],['The installed default convention includes smoothing and additive one, then normalizes each document vector to unit L2 length.','A log-base change alone does not account for the default formula and normalization.','The shown vectorizer performs term weighting, not POS tagging.','Documents remain the three command strings.'],conflict=TFI,cites=[(1455,'equation','Length-normalized TF'),(1456,'equation','Unsmoothed IDF'),(1457,'code','Default TfidfVectorizer and rounded table')],primary=[TFAPI,nb(19)])
ask(1457,'tfidf-code',CORP+' Under sklearn defaults idf=ln((1+N)/(1+df))+1 and L2 normalization, approximately what are d1’s ball and red components?', ['0.49048 and 0.37302','0.21972 and 0.08109','1.69315 and 1.28768','0.23776 and 0.18088'],['Rare terms ball/chair/near have IDF a=1+ln2; pick/red have b=1+ln(4/3). Dividing by sqrt(3a²+2b²) gives the pair.','These are the printed unsmoothed length-normalized formula values, not the stipulated code convention.','These are unnormalized IDF-weighted counts before L2 division.','These approximate division by the sum of weights, an L1 rather than L2 normalization.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['N=3; df(ball)=1 and df(red)=2.','Let a=1+ln(4/2)=1.693147 and b=1+ln(4/3)=1.287682.','d1 has three a-valued components and two b-valued components.','Divide ball a and red b by sqrt(3a²+2b²), obtaining .490479 and .373022.'],formulas=[SKTF],conflict=TFI,cites=[(1454,'code','Training corpus'),(1457,'code','TfidfVectorizer default call'),(1457,'table','First row rounded weights')],primary=[TFAPI,nb(19)])
ask(1457,'tfidf-code','The exact nonzero TF-IDF rows have L2 norm 1. What does this imply about their entries?', ['Their squared entries sum to 1; their ordinary entries need not sum to 1','Their ordinary entries sum to 1, so each row is a next-word probability distribution','Each nonzero entry equals the reciprocal of the vocabulary size','Their two-decimal printed entries must still have squared sum exactly 1'],['L2 normalization constrains the sum of squares of the unrounded vector, not its ordinary sum.','That confuses vector normalization with probability normalization.','Weights still differ with counts and document frequencies.','Rounding can perturb the exact norm.'],kind='conceptual',formulas=[fm('||x||₂=√(Σ_j x_j²)=1',{'x':'Exact nonzero normalized TF-IDF row','j':'Feature index'},{'x_j,||x||₂':'dimensionless'},['Use unrounded values and L2 normalization.'])],cites=[(1457,'table','Rounded TF-IDF output rows')],primary=[TFAPI])
